<!--
  Species and the kinds that spawn as them (docs/design/29-animals.md §1).

  A species is what a pawn IS: whether it is a person, how it traverses the graph, how fast it
  walks against the colonist's standard, how far it wanders and how long it rests. A kind is what
  SPAWNS, and names a species. The colonist is a species like any other, with `person` set.

  Kind order is a save contract: PawnContent.FromDefs lists the kinds by name and that list is
  the order. Append; never insert.
-->
<Defs>

  <SpeciesDef>
    <defName>Species_Person</defName>
    <labelKey>ui.pawn.colonist</labelKey>
    <person>true</person>
    <bodyLengthMm>2490</bodyLengthMm>
    <movePerMille>1000</movePerMille>
    <traverseMode>Colonist</traverseMode>
    <!-- The colonist's wander is the mental break's, in Break_Wander; these two are unread for a person. -->
    <wanderRadius>6</wanderRadius>
    <restTicksMin>0</restTicksMin>
    <restTicksMax>0</restTicksMax>
    <figureKey></figureKey>
    <!--
      Combat (design 33 section 1; owner, 2026-09-23): 100 hit points, downed at nought, dead at
      half the pool below it. A person has no natural attack - bare hands are Combat.xml's fists -
      and no revenge chance or species melee level: a colonist's answer to being hit is her
      faction's and her Melee skill's.
    -->
    <healthPoints>100</healthPoints>
    <deathAtPerMille>-500</deathAtPerMille>
    <!--
      Health (docs/design/43-health.md section 2): a person has a body of six regions over the
      pool. The animals name none and keep the pool alone (section 8).
    -->
    <health>Health_Person</health>
    <!-- A bullet crossing a person's cell takes it 400 times in a thousand before the dead zone
         (design 47 section 2c): the reference's 40 % at a person's size. -->
    <interceptPerMille>400</interceptPerMille>
  </SpeciesDef>

  <!--
    The midden hog: pig-descended, thrives on refuse heaps, the reliable meat animal
    (docs/design/proper-nouns.csv, creature.scavenger). Wild first; livestock is a later unit.

    An ambler: 600 per mille of the colonist's walk (was 700; owner, 2026-09-22: the trot read
    as "way too fast"). A hog never takes a ladder, cannot open a door and does not swim, which
    is TraverseMode.Animal. Rests are
    INVENTED: five to fifteen seconds between legs at sixty ticks a second, a radius of eight
    cells so a hog drifts rather than paces. All three are playtest numbers.
  -->
  <SpeciesDef>
    <defName>Species_MiddenHog</defName>
    <labelKey>ui.pawn.hog</labelKey>
    <person>false</person>
    <bodyLengthMm>1200</bodyLengthMm>
    <movePerMille>600</movePerMille>
    <traverseMode>Animal</traverseMode>
    <wanderRadius>8</wanderRadius>
    <restTicksMin>300</restTicksMin>
    <restTicksMax>900</restTicksMax>
    <figureKey>animal.hog</figureKey>
    <!--
      Combat (design 33 section 1). The pool and the revenge are the owner's: 60 hit points, and a
      hog usually turns on whoever hurt it - 700 in a thousand on every hit, else it runs. The
      tusks and the melee level are INVENTED: a heavy animal that hits hard and slowly, about as
      able as a colonist of level 6, which makes a hog a real fight for an unarmed novice.
    -->
    <healthPoints>60</healthPoints>
    <deathAtPerMille>-500</deathAtPerMille>
    <revengePerMille>700</revengePerMille>
    <meleeSkill>6</meleeSkill>
    <!-- A hog is a bigger body than a person on the line: 500 (design 47, the reference's
         size scaling). -->
    <interceptPerMille>500</interceptPerMille>
    <naturalAttack>
      <damage>6</damage>
      <cooldownTicks>150</cooldownTicks>
      <windupTicks>30</windupTicks>
      <damageKind>Blunt</damageKind>
      <style>Bite</style>
    </naturalAttack>
  </SpeciesDef>

  <!--
    The duct rat (proper-nouns.csv, creature.vermin — proposed). Vermin, small game, the walking
    proof of the figure path, and a threat once there is a health model to bite.

    Lively: 900 per mille. Climbs anything a colonist can, but does not swim (owner, 2026-09-22:
    no animal does by default), which is TraverseMode.Climber. Short rests and a short radius: a
    rat darts and stops. INVENTED, playtest numbers.
  -->
  <SpeciesDef>
    <defName>Species_DuctRat</defName>
    <nocturnal>true</nocturnal>
    <labelKey>ui.pawn.rat</labelKey>
    <person>false</person>
    <bodyLengthMm>250</bodyLengthMm>
    <movePerMille>900</movePerMille>
    <traverseMode>Climber</traverseMode>
    <wanderRadius>5</wanderRadius>
    <restTicksMin>120</restTicksMin>
    <restTicksMax>480</restTicksMax>
    <figureKey>animal.rat</figureKey>
    <!--
      Combat (design 33 section 1). The owner's: 15 hit points, and a rat usually runs - 50 in a
      thousand turns, the rest flee. The bite and the level are INVENTED: quick and weak, a nip
      every second and a half.
    -->
    <healthPoints>15</healthPoints>
    <deathAtPerMille>-500</deathAtPerMille>
    <revengePerMille>50</revengePerMille>
    <meleeSkill>3</meleeSkill>
    <!-- A rat is hardly there: 40, the reference's floor. -->
    <interceptPerMille>40</interceptPerMille>
    <naturalAttack>
      <damage>2</damage>
      <cooldownTicks>90</cooldownTicks>
      <windupTicks>15</windupTicks>
      <damageKind>Sharp</damageKind>
      <style>Bite</style>
    </naturalAttack>
  </SpeciesDef>

  <!--
    The culvert frog (proper-nouns.csv, creature.amphibian — proposed; docs/design/30-wildlife.md
    section 8). Lives on the banks of the streams, ponds and lakes and never strays far from them:
    bankRadius holds every leg it takes to within three cells of water, and a frog that finds
    itself further out heads back. It hops (presentation: the Jump clip is its gait).

    It does not swim. A frog would, but a swimming animal is a sixth traverse mode, and every mode
    costs a district flood on every nav rebuild whether any pawn walks in it or not (design 33
    section 16b); it is TraverseMode.Animal, the hog's - no ladders, no doors, no water - and sits
    at the water's edge instead. It does not mind the rain (ignoresRain): it is the one animal
    that stays out in it.

    Drawn about 0.87 m nose to toe - well over life beside a 2.49 m colonist, and deliberately:
    0.4 m could not be picked out from the play camera (owner, 2026-09-26: "just over double the
    size"). Pace 1,000 per mille since the same day, so that the bigger frog's hop is about 1.3 m,
    a body and a half, rather than the one metre it covered at 0.4 m. A radius of four, short
    rests. INVENTED, playtest numbers. A frog never turns on
    whoever hurts it (revenge 0) and so carries no attack.
  -->
  <SpeciesDef>
    <defName>Species_CulvertFrog</defName>
    <labelKey>ui.pawn.frog</labelKey>
    <person>false</person>
    <bodyLengthMm>870</bodyLengthMm>
    <movePerMille>1000</movePerMille>
    <traverseMode>Animal</traverseMode>
    <wanderRadius>4</wanderRadius>
    <restTicksMin>180</restTicksMin>
    <restTicksMax>720</restTicksMax>
    <bankRadius>3</bankRadius>
    <ignoresRain>true</ignoresRain>
    <!-- A group's frogs set off in different directions (owner, 2026-09-26): a new hop prefers a
         heading 60 degrees or more from every other frog within six cells that is already hopping. -->
    <divergeRadius>6</divergeRadius>
    <figureKey>animal.frog</figureKey>
    <healthPoints>8</healthPoints>
    <deathAtPerMille>-500</deathAtPerMille>
    <revengePerMille>0</revengePerMille>
    <meleeSkill>1</meleeSkill>
    <interceptPerMille>40</interceptPerMille>
  </SpeciesDef>

  <!--
    The butcher (docs/design/62-pig-butcher.md): a hulking hostile person with a cleaver, the boss a
    colony fights together, in four levels (section 4b; owner, 2026-09-26: "different levels based
    on their appearance ... a bit bigger each level"). A level is the pack's own colourway of the
    same giant - 01_A to 01_D - and is bigger, harder to kill and harder hitting than the one
    before. A person species of its own, not the colonist's, because what makes it a boss is what
    it IS: its pool, its body, its fixed melee level, its cleaver and its sweep. Every number is
    INVENTED and the first tuning (section 4).

    It is one cell, drawn huge (section 3): its bulk is TraverseMode.Animal - no ladder, no door it
    opens, no water - so a closed door is a wall it breaks and a ladder is a way to get away from it.
    A bigger body on a bullet's line than anything (800, the ceiling). Never stunned, never knocked
    back. The cleaver is its own, not an item: it does not drop, and nobody else swings it
    (section 5). The sweep (sections 5 and 7): the front arc of three, and a landed blow usually
    flings its victim, slamming into what stops it and falling off a ledge.
  -->
  <SpeciesDef Name="ButcherBase" Abstract="true">
    <person>true</person>
    <traverseMode>Animal</traverseMode>
    <wanderRadius>6</wanderRadius>
    <restTicksMin>0</restTicksMin>
    <restTicksMax>0</restTicksMax>
    <deathAtPerMille>-500</deathAtPerMille>
    <interceptPerMille>800</interceptPerMille>
    <unstoppable>true</unstoppable>
    <!-- The pig's voice (design 62 section 8d): a grunt as it swings, a squeal when it is hit, a
         bellow when it throws somebody, an oink as it goes down. Presentation only. -->
    <voice>butcher</voice>
  </SpeciesDef>

